registerRefData('nsw-cond-n-7703',{"id":7703,"pan":null,"ref":"10.2026.00000107.001","address":"68 Willoughby Road, Crows Nest  Nsw  2065","description":"Development Application to extend the Business Hours of Operation to 9am to 11pm Monday to Sunday and the Installation and Display of associated Business Identification Signage at an approved Food and Drinks Premises","outcome":"approved","outcome_raw":"Delegated Approved","outcome_source":"tracker","determined":"2026-04-22","notice_url":"https://masterview.northsydney.nsw.gov.au/document/download?key=folder-10778139&fileName=Document Set 10778139.pdf","sha256":"99970b5d5d86bf0e173e0e621c8f463cfb3387aec4e7cff9c479054e3ddcdfcd","pages":111,"layout":"numbered","n":52,"fetched_at":"2026-08-24T02:23:58+00:00","file":"n/7703.js","source_page":"https://masterview.northsydney.nsw.gov.au/Application/ApplicationDetails/010.2026.00000107.001","lga":"NORTH SYDNEY","conditions":[{"label":"1","code":null,"number":"1","title":"Protection of adjoining areas","text":"A temporary hoarding or temporary construction site fence must be erected between the work site and adjoining lands\nbefore the works begin and must be kept in place until after the completion of the works if the works -\n(a) could cause a danger, obstruction or inconvenience to pedestrian or vehicular traffic, or\n(b) could cause damage to adjoining lands by falling objects, or\n(c) involve the enclosure of a public place or part of a public place.\nNote.\nClauses 2.67 and 2.68 of this Policy specify which scaffolding, hoardings and temporary construction site fences are\nexempt development and state the applicable standards for that development.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":20},{"label":"2","code":null,"number":"2","title":"Toilet facilities","text":"(1) Toilet facilities must be available or provided at the work site before works begin and must be maintained until the\nworks are completed at a ratio of one toilet plus one additional toilet for every 20 persons employed at the site.\n(2) Each toilet must-\nAllCert P/L\nABN 23 657 810 627\nCDC-260053-01\nlimited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards legislation.\nSuite 8, 38 Rowe St,\nEASTWOOD NSW 2122\n(p): +61 2 9145 3959\n(a) be a standard flushing toilet connected to a public sewer, or\n(b) have an on-site effluent disposal system approved under the Local Government Act 1993, or\n(c) be a temporary chemical closet approved under the Local Government Act 1993.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":20},{"label":"3","code":null,"number":"3","title":"Waste management","text":"(1) A waste management plan for the work must be submitted to the principal certifying authority at least 2 days\nbefore work commences on the site\n(2) The waste management plan must-\n(a) identify all waste (including excavation, demolition and construction waste materials) that will be generated by the\nwork on the site, and\n(b) identify the quantity of waste material in tonnes and cubic metres to be-\n(i) reused on-site, and\n(ii) recycled on-site and off-site, and\n(iii) disposed of off-site, and\n(c) if waste materials are to be reused or recycled on-site-specify how the waste material will be reused or recycled\non-site, and\n(d) if waste materials are to be disposed of or recycled off-site-specify the contractor who will be transporting the\nmaterials and the waste facility or recycling outlet to which the materials will be taken.\n(3) A garbage receptacle must be provided at the work site before works begin and must be maintained until the\nworks are completed.\n(4) The garbage receptacle must have a tight fitting lid and be suitable for the reception of food scraps and papers.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":21},{"label":"4","code":null,"number":"4","title":"Adjoining wall dilapidation report","text":"(1) Before commencing any demolition or excavation works, the person having the benefit of the complying\ndevelopment certificate must obtain a dilapidation report on any part of a building that is within 2m of the works.\n(2) If the person preparing the report is denied access to the building for the purpose of an inspection, the report may\nbe prepared from an external inspection.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":21},{"label":"5","code":null,"number":"5","title":"Run-off and erosion controls","text":"Run-off and erosion controls must be implemented to prevent soil erosion, water pollution or the discharge of loose\nsediment on the surrounding land by-\n(a) diverting uncontaminated run-off around cleared or disturbed areas, and\n(b) erecting a silt fence and providing any other necessary sediment control measures that will prevent debris\nescaping into drainage systems, waterways or adjoining properties, and\n(c) preventing the tracking of sediment by vehicles onto roads, and\n(d) stockpiling top soil, excavated materials, construction and landscaping supplies and debris within the lot.\nPart 2 Conditions applying during the works\nAllCert P/L\nABN 23 657 810 627\nCDC-260053-01\nlimited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards legislation.\nSuite 8, 38 Rowe St,\nEASTWOOD NSW 2122\n(p): +61 2 9145 3959\nNote.\nThe Protection of the Environment Operations Act 1997 and the Protection of the Environment Operations (Noise\nControl) Regulation 2008 contain provisions relating to noise.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":21},{"label":"6","code":null,"number":"6","title":"Standard hours for construction","text":"Construction may only be carried out between 7.00 am and 6.00 pm on Monday to Friday, or between 8.00 am and\n1.00 pm on Saturdays, and no construction is to be carried out at any time on a Sunday or a public holiday.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":22},{"label":"7","code":null,"number":"7","title":"Works outside standard hours for construction","text":"(1) Work may be carried out outside the standard hours for construction if the work only generates noise that is -\n(a) no louder than 5 dB(A) above the rating background level at any adjoining residence in accordance with\nthe Interim Construction Noise Guideline (ISBN 978 1 74232 217 9) published by the Department of Environment and\nClimate Change NSW in July 2009, and\n(b) no louder than the noise management levels specified in Table 3 of that guideline at other sensitive receivers.\n(2) Work may be carried out outside the standard hours for construction-\n(a) for the delivery of materials-if prior approval has been obtained from the NSW Police Force or any other relevant\npublic authority, or\n(b) in an emergency, to avoid the loss of lives or property or to prevent environmental harm.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":22},{"label":"8","code":null,"number":"8","title":"Compliance with plans","text":"Works must be carried out in accordance with the plans and specifications to which the complying development\ncertificate relates.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":22},{"label":"9","code":null,"number":"9","title":"Demolition","text":"Any demolition must be carried out in accordance with AS 2601-2001, The demolition of structures.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":22},{"label":"10","code":null,"number":"10","title":"Maintenance of site","text":"(1) All materials and equipment must be stored wholly within the work site unless an approval to store them\nelsewhere is held.\n(2) Waste materials (including excavation, demolition and construction waste materials) must be managed on the site\nand then disposed of at a waste management facility.\n(3) Copies of receipts stating the following must be given to the principal certifying authority -\n(a) the place to which waste materials were transported,\n(b) the name of the contractor transporting the materials,\n(c) the quantity of materials transported off-site and recycled or disposed of.\n(4) Any run-off and erosion control measures required must be maintained within their operating capacity until the\ncompletion of the works to prevent debris escaping from the site into drainage systems, waterways, adjoining\nproperties and roads.\n(5) During construction-\nAllCert P/L\nABN 23 657 810 627\nCDC-260053-01\nlimited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards legislation.\nSuite 8, 38 Rowe St,\nEASTWOOD NSW 2122\n(p): +61 2 9145 3959\n(a) all vehicles entering or leaving the site must have their loads covered, and\n(b) all vehicles, before leaving the site, must be cleaned of dirt, sand and other materials, to avoid tracking these\nmaterials onto public roads.\n(6) At the completion of the works, the work site must be left clear of waste and debris.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":22},{"label":"11","code":null,"number":"11","title":"Earthworks, retaining walls and structural support","text":"(1) Any earthworks (including any structural support or other related structure for the purposes of the development) -\n(a) must not cause a danger to life or property or damage to any adjoining building or structure on the lot or to any\nbuilding or structure on any adjoining lot, and\n(b) must not redirect the flow of any surface or ground water or cause sediment to be transported onto an adjoining\nproperty, and\n(c) that is fill brought to the site-must contain only virgin excavated natural material (VENM) as defined in Part 3 of\nSchedule 1 to the Protection of the Environment Operations Act 1997, and\n(d) that is excavated soil to be removed from the site-must be disposed of in accordance with any requirements\nunder the Protection of the Environment Operations (Waste) Regulation 2005.\n(2) Any excavation must be carried out in accordance with Excavation Work: Code of Practice (ISBN 978-0-642-\n785442 [PDF] and ISBN 978-0-642-785459 [DOCX]), published in July 2012 by Safe Work Australia.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":23},{"label":"12","code":null,"number":"12","title":"Drainage connections","text":"(1) If the work is the erection of, or an alteration or addition to, a building, the roof stormwater drainage system must\nbe installed and connected to the drainage system before the roof is installed.\n(2) Any approval that is required for connection to the drainage system under the Local Government Act 1993 must\nbe held before the connection is carried out.\n12A Contamination discovered during works\n(1) If during works on the land comprising the lot, the land is found to be contaminated, within the meaning of\nthe Contaminated Land Management Act 1997-\n(a) all works must stop immediately, and\n(b) the Environment Protection Authority and the council must be notified of the contamination.\n(2) Land is found to be contaminated for the purposes of this clause if the person having the benefit of the complying\ndevelopment certificate or the principal certifying authority knows or should reasonably suspect the land is\ncontaminated.\nNote-\nDepending on the nature and level of the contamination, remediation of the land may be required before further work\ncan continue.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":23},{"label":"13","code":null,"number":"13","title":"Archaeology discovered during works","text":"If any object having interest due to its age or association with the past is uncovered during the course of the work -\n(a) all work must stop immediately in that area, and\nAllCert P/L\nABN 23 657 810 627\nCDC-260053-01\nlimited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards legislation.\nSuite 8, 38 Rowe St,\nEASTWOOD NSW 2122\n(p): +61 2 9145 3959\n(b) the Office of Environment and Heritage must be advised of the discovery.\nNote.\nDepending on the significance of the object uncovered, an archaeological assessment and excavation permit under\nthe Heritage Act 1977 may be required before further the work can continue.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":23},{"label":"14","code":null,"number":"14","title":"Aboriginal objects discovered during works","text":"If any Aboriginal object (including evidence of habitation or remains) is discovered during the course of the work -\n(a) all excavation or disturbance of the area must stop immediately in that area, and\n(b) the Office of Environment and Heritage must be advised of the discovery in accordance with section 89A of\nthe National Parks and Wildlife Act 1974.\nNote.\nIf an Aboriginal object is discovered, an Aboriginal heritage impact permit may be required under the National Parks\nand Wildlife Act 1974.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":24},{"label":"15","code":null,"number":"15","title":"When a survey certificate is required","text":"(1) If any part of the work is the erection of a new building, or an alteration or addition to an existing building, that is\nlocated less than 3m from the lot boundary, a survey certificate must be given to the principal certifying authority -\n(a) before any form work below the ground floor slab is completed, or\n(b) if there is no such form work-before the concrete is poured for the ground floor slab.\n(2) The survey certificate must be prepared by a registered land surveyor and show the location of the work relative to\nthe boundaries of the site.\nPart 3 Conditions applying before the issue of an occupation certificate","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":24},{"label":"16","code":null,"number":"16","title":"Vehicular access","text":"If the work involves the construction of a vehicular access point, the access point must be completed before the\noccupation certificate for the work on the site is obtained.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":24},{"label":"17","code":null,"number":"17","title":"Utility services","text":"(1) If the work requires alteration to, or the relocation of, utility services on, or adjacent to, the lot on which the work is\ncarried out, the work is not complete until all such works are carried out.\n(2) If the work is the subject of a notice of requirements for water supply or sewerage services (or both) by a water\nutility or an entity authorised by the utility, the work must be satisfactorily completed and any monetary contributions\nrequired to be paid to the relevant water supply authority must be paid before the occupation certificate is issued.\n(3) If the work is the subject of a compliance certificate under section 73 of the Sydney Water Act 1994, the work\nmust be satisfactorily completed and any monetary contributions required to be paid to the Sydney Water Corporation\nmust be paid before the occupation certificate is issued.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":24},{"label":"18","code":null,"number":"18","title":"Mechanical ventilation systems","text":"If the work includes a mechanical ventilation system that is a regulated system within the meaning of the Public\nHealth Act 2010, the system must be notified as required by the Public Health Regulation 2012, before an occupation\ncertificate (whether interim or final) for the work is issued.\nAllCert P/L\nABN 23 657 810 627\nCDC-260053-01\nlimited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards legislation.\nSuite 8, 38 Rowe St,\nEASTWOOD NSW 2122\n(p): +61 2 9145 3959","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":24},{"label":"19","code":null,"number":"19","title":"Food businesses","text":"If the work relates to a food business within the meaning of the Food Act 2003, the food business must be notified as\nrequired by that Act, or licensed as required by the Food Regulation 2010, before an occupation certificate (whether\ninterim or final) for the work is issued.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":25},{"label":"20","code":null,"number":"20","title":"Premises where skin penetration procedures are carried out","text":"If the work relates to premises at which a skin penetration procedure, within the meaning of the Public Health Act\n2010, will be carried out, the premises must be notified as required by Part 4 of the Public Health Regulation\n2012 before an occupation certificate (whether interim or final) for the work is issued.\nPart 4 Operational requirements","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":25},{"label":"21","code":null,"number":"21","title":"Hours of operation","text":"(1) If there are existing conditions on a development consent applying to hours of operation, the development must\nnot be operated outside the hours specified in those conditions.\n(2) If there are no existing conditions on a development consent applying to hours of operation, the development must\nnot be operated outside the following hours-\n(a) if the development involves a new use as specialised retail premises or other commercial premises -7.00 am to\n10.00 pm Monday to Saturday and 7.00 am to 8.00 pm on a Sunday or a public holiday,\n(b) if the development involves a new use as something other than a specialised retail premises or other commercial\npremises and adjoins or is opposite a residential lot within a residential zone or Zone RU5 Village -7.00 am to 7.00\npm Monday to Saturday and no operation on a Sunday or a public holiday,\n(c) in any other case not referred to in paragraph (a) or (b)-7.00 am to 7.00 pm Monday to Saturday and 9.00 am to\n6.00 pm on a Sunday or a public holiday.","stage":"ongoing","stage_basis":"text-under-general-heading","page":25},{"label":"22","code":null,"number":"22","title":"Noise","text":"The development must comply with the Noise Policy.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":25},{"label":"23","code":null,"number":"23","title":"Lighting","text":"(1) All new external lighting must-\n(a) comply with AS/NZS 4282:2019, Control of the obtrusive effects of outdoor lighting, and\n(b) be mounted, screened and directed in a way that it does not create a nuisance or light spill on to buildings on\nadjoining lots or public places.\n(2) Lighting at vehicle access points to the development must be provided in accordance with the following\nstandards-\n(i) AS/NZS 1158.0:2005, Lighting for roads and public spaces, Part 0: Introduction,\n(ii) AS/NZS 1158.1.1:2005, Lighting for roads and public spaces, Part 1.1: Vehicular traffic (Category V) lighting -\nPerformance and design requirements,\n(iii) AS/NZS 1158.1.2:2010, Lighting for roads and public spaces, Part 1.2: Vehicular traffic (Category V) lighting -\nGuide to design, installation, operation and maintenance,\nAllCert P/L\nABN 23 657 810 627\nCDC-260053-01\nlimited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards legislation.\nSuite 8, 38 Rowe St,\nEASTWOOD NSW 2122\n(p): +61 2 9145 3959\n(iv) AS/NZS 1158.2:2020, Lighting for roads and public spaces, Part 2: Computer procedures for the calculation of\nlight technical parameters for Category V and Category P lighting,\n(v) AS/NZS 1158.3.1:2020, Lighting for roads and public spaces, Part 3.1: Pedestrian area (Category P) lighting -\nPerformance and design requirements,\n(vi) AS/NZS 1158.4:2015, Lighting for roads and public spaces, Part 4: Lighting of pedestrian crossings,\n(vii) AS/NZS 1158.5:2014, Lighting for roads and public spaces, Part 5: Tunnels and underpasses.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":25},{"label":"24","code":null,"number":"24","title":"Use of driveways and parking areas","text":"(1) All driveways and parking areas must be unobstructed at all times.\n(2) Except as otherwise permitted by an existing condition of the most recent development consent (other than a\ncomplying development certificate) that applies to the premises, driveways and car spaces -\n(a) must not be used for the manufacture, storage or display of goods, materials or any other equipment, and\n(b) must be used solely for vehicular access and for the parking of vehicles associated with the use of the premises.\n24A Loading and unloading of goods or materials\n(1) Loading and unloading goods or materials must not be carried out on a public road.\n(2) This condition does not apply to a complying development certificate for development involving a building with a\ngross floor area less than 500m2 in Zone E1, E2, MU1, B1, B2, B3, B4, B8 or SP5 that existed before 1 February\n2022.\n24B (Repealed)","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":26},{"label":"25","code":null,"number":"25","title":"Landscaped area (planting and maintenance)","text":"(1) Any tree or shrub that fails to establish within 2 years of the initial planting date must be replaced with the same\nspecies of tree or shrub.\n(2) All landscaped areas on the site must be maintained on an on-going basis.\nPart 5 Conditions applying to entertainment venues","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":26},{"label":"26","code":null,"number":"26","title":"Application of prescribed conditions of development consent","text":"The conditions of a development consent for the use of a building as an entertainment venue prescribed for the\npurposes of the Act, s 4.17(11) are conditions of a complying development certificate for the use of a building as an\nentertainment venue.\nNote-\nSee the Environmental Planning and Assessment Regulation 2021, section 72.\nCONSTRUCTIONCROW.NSWYO-CHICROWS NEST23/02/2026AQ\nSHOPFITTER TO CONFIRM ALL DIMENSIONS ONSITE PRIOR TO COMMENCING WORKS ANDNOTIFY DESIGNER OF ANY DISCREPANCIES.DRAWINGS ARE TO BE READ IN CONJUCTIONSWILL ALL RELEVANT SPECIFICATIONS, REPORTSAND APPROVALS.\nWWW.YOCHI.COM.AU435 CROWN STREETSURRY HILLS NSW 2010AUSTRALIACOPYRIGHT EMBRACE THECHIPTY LTD68 WILLOUGHBY ROAD,CROWS NEST, NSW 2065\nDATEREVAMMENDMENTPROJECT CODESCALEISSUEPROJECT\nPROJECT ADDRESS\nDRAWING TITLE\nSHEET NUMBERREV ISSUE DATEDRAWN BY10/02/2026CONSTRUCTION\nA\n23/02/2026CONSTRUCTIONA\nA001GENERAL NOTES 1NTS @ A3\nALL FRONT OF HOUSE EXISTING CONCRETE FLOORS TOBE GROUND. CONTRACTOR TO PROVIDE METHOD ANDRECOGNISED MATERIALS PROPOSED TO SEAL ANDPROTECT THE FLOOR.GRIND CONCRETE FLOOR TO REMOVE ANY GLUES ANDLIGHTLY EXPOSE AGGREGATE. FILL AND PATCH ALLDEFECTS, VOIDS AND CRACKS AND SEAL FLOOR WITHPROTECTIVE COMMERCIAL GRADE CLEAR MAT COATINGUSING RECOGNISED COATINGS.ENSURE COMPLIANT SLIP RESISTANCE IN MAINTAINEDTHROUGHOUT.PROVIDE SAMPLE FOR APPROVAL ON SITE PRIOR TOCOMPLETION. ON COMPLETION PROVIDE WARRANTIESAND MAINTENANCE AND CLEANING INSTRUCTION.\nCONCRETE FLOORCROSS REFERENCE GENERAL NOTESNEW DOORWAYS SHALL HAVE A MINIMUM LUMINANCECONTRAST OF 30% BETWEEN THE DOOR LEAF AND JAMBOR DOOR JAMB AND WALL TO BCA 2022 AMDT 2 SPEC. 15.DOOR HANDLES SHALL BE SUITABLE FOR PERSONSWITH A HAND IMPAIRMENT / D TYPE LEVERL CONFORMSTO AS1428.1. FORCE REQUIRED TO HOLD AND OPENDOOR TO WHICH DOOR CLOSER IS FITTED SHALL NOTEXCEED 20N.DOOR HARDWARE SHALL BE INSTALLED BETWEEN900-1100MM ABOVE FFL IN ACCORDANCE WITH AS1428.1SECT 13 FIG 36.EXIT DOORS SHALL BE OPERABLE WITHOUT A KEY FROMTHE INSIDE WITH A SINGLE HAND DOWNWARD ACTIONOR PUSHING ON A SINGLE DEVICE IN ACCORDANCEWITH BCA 2022 AMDT 2 D3D26.VISUAL INDICATORS SHALL BE PROVIDED TO ALLGLAZED DOORS OR WINDOWS WITHOUT A TRANSOM.GLAZING SHALL BE MARKED WITH A SOLID 30%CONTRASTING STANDARD DECAL LINE 75MM WIDELOCATED 900-1000MM AFL. REFER TO DRAWINGS\nDOORS & WINDOWS\n•••\nCROSS REFERENCE GENERAL NOTES, ELECTRICAL ANDLIGHTING.CONTRACTOR TO SUPPLY AND INSTALL ALL REQUIREDFIRE SAFETY EQUIPMENT IN ACCORDANCE WITH NCCAND AS2444-2001 REGULATIONS.KITCHEN AREAS NOT CONTAINING COOKING OILS ANDFATS MUST BE FITTED WITH THE FOLLOWING TYPE OFEXTINGUISHER3AB(E) AND FIRE BLANKETADDITIONAL TO ABOVE:NO POINT ON THE FLOOR WITHIN THE BUILDINGSHOULD BE GREATER THAN 15M TRAVEL DISTANCETO A SINGLE EXTINGUISHER OR 30M BETWEEN TWOFIRE EXTINGUISHERS.IF AN ELECTRICAL SWITCHBOARD SUPPLIES POWERTO A SERVICE DURING AN EMERGENCY A FIREEXTINGUISHER TYPE AB(E) MUST BE WITHIN 2M OFTHE ELECTRICAL SWITCHBOARDFIRE EXTINGUISHERS ARE TO BE SUPPORTED ON WALLBRACKETS APPROXIMATELY 1M ABOVE FLOOR HEIGHT.FIRE EXTINGUISHER SIGN TO BE MOUNTED ABOVE FIREEXTINGUISHER AT MIN 2M ABOVE FLOOR HEIGHT OR ONOPENING FRONTAL.EARLY FIRE HAZARDS INDICE (CLAUSE C2D11 & SPEC 7)ALL NEW FIXED SURFACE FINISHES TO COMPLY WITHTHE FOLLOWING FIRE HAZARD INDICES:SPREAD OF FLAME INDEX <=9SMOKE DEVELOPED INDEX <=8 IF THE SPREAD OF FLAMEIS MORE THAN 5 OR FLOOR MATERIALS AND FLOORCOVERINGS. HAVE A CRITICAL RADIANT HEAT FLUX (CRF)NOT LESS THAN 1.2KW/M2. WALL AND CEILING LINING MATERIALS MUST BE GROUPNUMBER 1, 2 OR 3 AS REQUIRED BY BCA 2022 AMDT 2 SPECIFICATIONSPEC. 7.FIRE SERVICES COMMISSIONING TEST REPORT ANDCERTIFICATION TO BE PROVIDED BY CONTRACTOR FORTHE INSTALLATION/AMENDMENT OF ALL SERVICESPRIOR TO PRACTICAL COMPLETION.\nFIRE & EMERGENCY\n••••••••••\nALL WORKS ARE TO BE IN ACCORDANCE WITH THE NCC,AUSTRALIAN STANDARDS, STATUTORY REGULATIONSAND LOCAL AUTHORITY REQUIREMENTS.ALL CONTRACTORS ARE TO COMPLY WITH THECURRENT HEALTH AND SAFETY REGULATIONS AND OH&SPRACTICES AT ALL TIMES.CHECK ALL DIMENSIONS ON SITE AGAINST WORKINGDRAWINGS BEFORE COMMENCING ON SITE. NOASSUMPTIONS TO BE MADE IN READING OF DRAWINGS.ANY DISCREPENCIES WITHIN DRAWINGS, SCHEDULES,SPECIFICATIONS ARE TO BE CLARIFIED WITH THEDESIGNER PRIOR TO CONSTRUCTION.VARIATIONS TO PLANS AND/OR SPECIFIED FINISHES MAYNOT BE MADE WITHOUT OBTAINING WRITTEN CONSENTFROM THE DESIGNER.A LEGIBLE SET OF CURRENT DRAWINGS MUST BEMAINTAINED ON SITE AT ALL TIMES.BUILDER IS RESPONSIBLE FOR OBTAINING A COPY OFTHE TENANCY FIT OUT GUIDELINES IF APPLICABLE, ANDCONDITIONS OF APPROVAL.COMPLY WITH REQUIREMENTS, REFER TO DESIGNERFOR ANY CONTRADICTING GUIDLINES.ALL SITE WORKS AND DEFECTS ARE TO BE COMPLIANTTO AUSTRALIAN STANDARDS AND RECTIFIED TO THESATISFACTION OF THE CLIENT AND DESIGNER.PREPARE AND MAKE GOOD TO ALL SURFACES ANDSUBSTRATE AS REQUIRED TO RECIEVE NEW FINISHESAS PER THE MANUFACTURER WRITTEN SPECIFICATION.PROVIDE NEW WATERPROOF MEMBRANE FOR ALL WETAREAS AND AS REQUIRED FOR ALL FLOORS AND WALLSTO COMPLY WITH NATIONAL FOOD CODES.CONTRACTOR TO ENSURE CONSISTENCY BETWEENMATERIALS IS MAINTAINED.ALL FIXTURES, FINISHES AND EQUIPMENT IS TO BEINSTALLED ACCORDING TO MANUFACTURERINSTRUCTIONS.USE ADHESIVES FASTENERS AND FIXINGS CAPABLE OFTRANSMITTING THE LOADS IMPOSED AND ENSURE THERIGIDITY OF THE ASSEMBLY. CONCEAL ALL FIXINGS ANDSCREQS UNLESS INSIDE CUPBOARDS AND CAP ALLFIXINGS INSIDE CUPBOARDS.THE CONTRACTOR IS RESPONSIBLE FOR ALLCERTIFICATE OF COMPLIANCE, FORM 2'S, PART A AND BSTATEMENTS, O&M MANUALS, OCCUPANCYCERTIFICATES AND DRAWINGS SHOWING BUILDINGSERVICES POSITIONS PRIOR TO PRACTICALCOMPLETION.ALL WORKS TO BE CARRIED OUT IN ACCORDANCE WITH:AS 4674-2004 DESIGN, CONSTRUCTION AND FITOUTOF FOOD PREMISESFSANZ FOOD STANDARDS CODETHE FOOD ACT 2003THE FOOD ACT REGULATION 2015AS 1288.1-2021, AS 2047 AND AS 1170.1 GLAZING(INCLUDING DECALS)LOCAL COUNCIL REGULATIONS FOR WASTEMANAGEMENTALL RELEVANT TOWN PLANNING CONSENTCONDITIONSTHE RELEVANT PROVISIONS OF THE BCA 2022 AMDT 2 RELATINGTOMECHANICAL VENTILATIONTOILET AND OTHER SANITARY FACILITIES FORPATRONS AND STAFFAS 4674-2004","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":26},{"label":"1","code":null,"number":"1","title":"ProtecƟon of adjoining areas","text":"A temporary hoarding or temporary construcƟon site fence must be erected between the\nwork site and adjoining lands before the works begin and must be kept in place unƟl aŌer the\ncompleƟon of the works if the works:\n(a) could cause a danger, obstrucƟon or inconvenience to pedestrian or vehicular\ntraﬃc, or\n(b) could cause damage to adjoining lands by falling objects, or\n(c) involve the enclosure of a public place or part of a public place.\nNote. Clauses 2.67 and 2.68 of this Policy specify which scaﬀolding, hoardings and temporary construcƟon site\nfences are exempt development and state the applicable standards for that development.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":93},{"label":"2","code":null,"number":"2","title":"Toilet faciliƟes","text":"(1) Toilet faciliƟes must be available or provided at the work site before works begin and must\nbe maintained unƟl the works are completed at a raƟo of one toilet plus one addiƟonal toilet\nfor every 20 persons employed at the site.\n(2) Each toilet must-\n(a) be a standard ﬂushing toilet connected to a public sewer, or\n39 (b) have an on-site eﬄuent disposal system approved under the Local Government\nAct 1993, or\n(c) be a temporary chemical closet approved under the Local Government Act 1993.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":93},{"label":"3","code":null,"number":"3","title":"Waste management","text":"(1) A waste management plan for the work must be submiƩed to the principal cerƟfying\nauthority at least 2 days before work commences on the site\n(2) The waste management plan must-\n(a) idenƟfy all waste (including excavaƟon, demoliƟon and construcƟon waste\nmaterials) that will be generated by the work on the site, and\n(b) idenƟfy the quanƟty of waste material in tonnes and cubic metres to be-\n(i) reused on-site, and\n(ii) recycled on-site and oﬀ-site, and\n(iii) disposed of oﬀ-site, and\n(c) if waste materials are to be reused or recycled on-site-specify how the waste\nmaterial will be reused or recycled on-site, and\n(d) if waste materials are to be disposed of or recycled oﬀ-site-specify the contractor\nwho will be transporƟng the materials and the waste facility or recycling outlet to\nwhich the materials will be taken.\n(3) A garbage receptacle must be provided at the work site before works begin and must be\nmaintained unƟl the works are completed.\n(4) The garbage receptacle must have a Ɵght ﬁƫng lid and be suitable for the recepƟon of\nfood scraps and papers.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":94},{"label":"4","code":null,"number":"4","title":"Adjoining wall dilapidaƟon report","text":"(1) Before commencing any demoliƟon or excavaƟon works, the person having the beneﬁt of\nthe complying development cerƟﬁcate must obtain a dilapidaƟon report on any part of a\nbuilding that is within 2m of the works.\n(2) If the person preparing the report is denied access to the building for the purpose of an\ninspecƟon, the report may be prepared from an external inspecƟon.\n40","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":94},{"label":"5","code":null,"number":"5","title":"Run-oﬀ and erosion controls","text":"Run-oﬀ and erosion controls must be implemented to prevent soil erosion, water polluƟon or\nthe discharge of loose sediment on the surrounding land by-\n(a) diverƟng uncontaminated run-oﬀ around cleared or disturbed areas, and\n(b) erecƟng a silt fence and providing any other necessary sediment control measures\nthat will prevent debris escaping into drainage systems, waterways or adjoining\nproperƟes, and\n(c) prevenƟng the tracking of sediment by vehicles onto roads, and\n(d) stockpiling top soil, excavated materials, construcƟon and landscaping supplies\nand debris within the lot.\nPART 2 CONDITIONS APPLYING DURING THE WORKS\nNote: The ProtecƟon of the Environment OperaƟons Act 1997 and the ProtecƟon of the Environment\nOperaƟons (Noise Control) RegulaƟon 2008 contain provisions relaƟng to noise.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":95},{"label":"6","code":null,"number":"6","title":"Standard hours for construcƟon","text":"ConstrucƟon may only be carried out between 7.00 am and 6.00 pm on Monday to Friday, or\nbetween 8.00 am and 1.00 pm on Saturdays, and no construcƟon is to be carried out at any\nƟme on a Sunday or a public holiday.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":95},{"label":"7","code":null,"number":"7","title":"Works outside standard hours for construcƟon","text":"(1) Work may be carried out outside the standard hours for construcƟon if the work only\ngenerates noise that is-\n(a) no louder than 5 dB(A) above the raƟng background level at any adjoining\nresidence in accordance with the Interim ConstrucƟon Noise Guideline (ISBN 978 1\n74232 217 9) published by the Department of Environment and Climate Change NSW\nin July 2009, and\n(b) no louder than the noise management levels speciﬁed in Table 3 of that guideline\nat other sensiƟve receivers.\n(2) Work may be carried out outside the standard hours for construcƟon-\n(a) for the delivery of materials-if prior approval has been obtained from the NSW\nPolice Force or any other relevant public authority, or\n(b) in an emergency, to avoid the loss of lives or property or to prevent environmental\nharm.\n41","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":95},{"label":"8","code":null,"number":"8","title":"Compliance with plans","text":"Works must be carried out in accordance with the plans and speciﬁcaƟons to which the\ncomplying development cerƟﬁcate relates.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":96},{"label":"9","code":null,"number":"9","title":"DemoliƟon","text":"Any demoliƟon must be carried out in accordance with AS 2601-2001, The demoliƟon of\nstructures.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":96},{"label":"10","code":null,"number":"10","title":"Maintenance of site","text":"(1) All materials and equipment must be stored wholly within the work site unless an approval\nto store them elsewhere is held.\n(2) Waste materials (including excavaƟon, demoliƟon and construcƟon waste materials) must\nbe managed on the site and then disposed of at a waste management facility.\n(3) Copies of receipts staƟng the following must be given to the principal cerƟfying\nauthority-\n(a) the place to which waste materials were transported,\n(b) the name of the contractor transporƟng the materials,\n(c) the quanƟty of materials transported oﬀ-site and recycled or disposed of.\n(4) Any run-oﬀ and erosion control measures required must be maintained within their\noperaƟng capacity unƟl the compleƟon of the works to prevent debris escaping from the site\ninto drainage systems, waterways, adjoining properƟes and roads.\n(5) During construcƟon-\n(a) all vehicles entering or leaving the site must have their loads covered, and\n(b) all vehicles, before leaving the site, must be cleaned of dirt, sand and other\nmaterials, to avoid tracking these materials onto public roads.\n(6) At the compleƟon of the works, the work site must be leŌ clear of waste and debris.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":96},{"label":"11","code":null,"number":"11","title":"Earthworks, retaining walls and structural support","text":"(1) Any earthworks (including any structural support or other related structure for the\npurposes of the development)-\n(a) must not cause a danger to life or property or damage to any adjoining building or\nstructure on the lot or to any building or structure on any adjoining lot, and\n(b) must not redirect the ﬂow of any surface or ground water or cause sediment to be\ntransported onto an adjoining property, and\n42 (c) that is ﬁll brought to the site-must contain only virgin excavated natural material\n(VENM) as deﬁned in Part 3 of Schedule 1 to the ProtecƟon of the Environment\nOperaƟons Act 1997, and\n(d) that is excavated soil to be removed from the site-must be disposed of in\naccordance with any requirements under the ProtecƟon of the Environment\nOperaƟons (Waste) RegulaƟon 2005.\n(2) Any excavaƟon must be carried out in accordance with ExcavaƟon Work: Code of PracƟce\n(ISBN 978-0-642-785442 [PDF] and ISBN 978-0-642-785459 [DOCX]), published in July 2012\nby Safe Work Australia.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":96},{"label":"12","code":null,"number":"12","title":"Drainage connecƟons","text":"(1) If the work is the erecƟon of, or an alteraƟon or addiƟon to, a building, the roof stormwater\ndrainage system must be installed and connected to the drainage system before the roof is\ninstalled.\n(2) Any approval that is required for connecƟon to the drainage system under the Local\nGovernment Act 1993 must be held before the connecƟon is carried out.\n12A ContaminaƟon discovered during works\n(1) If during works on the land comprising the lot, the land is found to be contaminated, within\nthe meaning of the Contaminated Land Management Act 1997-\n(a) all works must stop immediately, and\n(b) the Environment ProtecƟon Authority and the council must be noƟﬁed of the\ncontaminaƟon.\n(2) Land is found to be contaminated for the purposes of this clause if the person having the\nbeneﬁt of the complying development cerƟﬁcate or the principal cerƟfying authority knows\nor should reasonably suspect the land is contaminated.\nNote: Depending on the nature and level of the contaminaƟon, remediaƟon of the land may be required before\nfurther work can conƟnue.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":97},{"label":"13","code":null,"number":"13","title":"Archaeology discovered during works","text":"If any object having interest due to its age or associaƟon with the past is uncovered during the\ncourse of the work-\n(a) all work must stop immediately in that area, and\n(b) the Oﬃce of Environment and Heritage must be advised of the discovery.\nNote: Depending on the signiﬁcance of the object uncovered, an archaeological assessment and excavaƟon\npermit under the Heritage Act 1977 may be required before further the work can conƟnue.\n43","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":97},{"label":"14","code":null,"number":"14","title":"Aboriginal objects discovered during works","text":"If any Aboriginal object (including evidence of habitaƟon or remains) is discovered during the\ncourse of the work-\n(a) all excavaƟon or disturbance of the area must stop immediately in that area, and\n(b) the Oﬃce of Environment and Heritage must be advised of the discovery in accordance\nwith secƟon 89A of the NaƟonal Parks and Wildlife Act 1974.\nNote: If an Aboriginal object is discovered, an Aboriginal heritage impact permit may be required under the\nNaƟonal Parks and Wildlife Act 1974.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":98},{"label":"15","code":null,"number":"15","title":"When a survey cerƟﬁcate is required","text":"(1) If any part of the work is the erecƟon of a new building, or an alteraƟon or addiƟon to an\nexisƟng building, that is located less than 3m from the lot boundary, a survey cerƟﬁcate must\nbe given to the principal cerƟfying authority-\n(a) before any form work below the ground ﬂoor slab is completed, or\n(b) if there is no such form work-before the concrete is poured for the ground ﬂoor\nslab.\n(2) The survey cerƟﬁcate must be prepared by a registered land surveyor and show the\nlocaƟon of the work relaƟve to the boundaries of the site.\nPART 3 CONDITIONS APPLYING BEFORE THE ISSUE OF AN OCCUPATION CERTIFICATE","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":98},{"label":"16","code":null,"number":"16","title":"Vehicular access","text":"If the work involves the construcƟon of a vehicular access point, the access point must be\ncompleted before the occupaƟon cerƟﬁcate for the work on the site is obtained.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":98},{"label":"17","code":null,"number":"17","title":"UƟlity services","text":"(1) If the work requires alteraƟon to, or the relocaƟon of, uƟlity services on, or adjacent to,\nthe lot on which the work is carried out, the work is not complete unƟl all such works are\ncarried out.\n(2) If the work is the subject of a noƟce of requirements for water supply or sewerage services\n(or both) by a water uƟlity or an enƟty authorised by the uƟlity, the work must be saƟsfactorily\ncompleted and any monetary contribuƟons required to be paid to the relevant water supply\nauthority must be paid before the occupaƟon cerƟﬁcate is issued.\n(3) If the work is the subject of a compliance cerƟﬁcate under secƟon 73 of the Sydney Water\nAct 1994, the work must be saƟsfactorily completed and any monetary contribuƟons required\nto be paid to the Sydney Water CorporaƟon must be paid before the occupaƟon cerƟﬁcate is\nissued.\n44","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":98},{"label":"18","code":null,"number":"18","title":"Mechanical venƟlaƟon systems","text":"If the work includes a mechanical venƟlaƟon system that is a regulated system within the\nmeaning of the Public Health Act 2010, the system must be noƟﬁed as required by the Public\nHealth RegulaƟon 2012, before an occupaƟon cerƟﬁcate (whether interim or ﬁnal) for the\nwork is issued.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":99},{"label":"19","code":null,"number":"19","title":"Food businesses","text":"If the work relates to a food business within the meaning of the Food Act 2003, the food\nbusiness must be noƟﬁed as required by that Act, or licensed as required by the Food\nRegulaƟon 2010, before an occupaƟon cerƟﬁcate (whether interim or ﬁnal) for the work is\nissued.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":99},{"label":"20","code":null,"number":"20","title":"Premises where skin penetraƟon procedures are carried out","text":"If the work relates to premises at which a skin penetraƟon procedure, within the meaning of\nthe Public Health Act 2010, will be carried out, the premises must be noƟﬁed as required by\nPart 4 of the Public Health RegulaƟon 2012 before an occupaƟon cerƟﬁcate (whether interim\nor ﬁnal) for the work is issued.\nPART 4 OPERATIONAL REQUIREMENTS","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":99},{"label":"21","code":null,"number":"21","title":"Hours of operaƟon","text":"(1) If there are exisƟng condiƟons on a development consent applying to hours of operaƟon,\nthe development must not be operated outside the hours speciﬁed in those condiƟons.\n(2) If there are no exisƟng condiƟons on a development consent applying to hours of\noperaƟon, the development must not be operated outside the following hours-\n(a) if the development involves a new use as specialised retail premises or other\ncommercial premises-7.00 am to 10.00 pm Monday to Saturday and 7.00 am to 8.00\npm on a Sunday or a public holiday,\n(b) if the development involves a new use as something other than a specialised retail\npremises or other commercial premises and adjoins or is opposite a residenƟal lot\nwithin a residenƟal zone or Zone RU5 Village-7.00 am to 7.00 pm Monday to Saturday\nand no operaƟon on a Sunday or a public holiday,\n(c) in any other case not referred to in paragraph (a) or (b)-7.00 am to 7.00 pm\nMonday to Saturday and 9.00 am to 6.00 pm on a Sunday or a public holiday.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":99},{"label":"22","code":null,"number":"22","title":"Noise","text":"The development must comply with the Noise Policy.\n45","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":99},{"label":"23","code":null,"number":"23","title":"LighƟng","text":"(1) All new external lighƟng must-\n(a) comply with AS/NZS 4282:2019, Control of the obtrusive eﬀects of outdoor\nlighƟng, and\n(b) be mounted, screened and directed in a way that it does not create a nuisance or\nlight spill on to buildings on adjoining lots or public places.\n(2) LighƟng at vehicle access points to the development must be provided in accordance with\nthe following standards-\n(i) AS/NZS 1158.0:2005, LighƟng for roads and public spaces, Part 0: IntroducƟon,\n(ii) AS/NZS 1158.1.1:2005, LighƟng for roads and public spaces, Part 1.1: Vehicular\ntraﬃc (Category V) lighƟng-Performance and design requirements,\n(iii) AS/NZS 1158.1.2:2010, LighƟng for roads and public spaces, Part 1.2: Vehicular\ntraﬃc (Category V) lighƟng-Guide to design, installaƟon, operaƟon and maintenance,\n(iv) AS/NZS 1158.2:2020, LighƟng for roads and public spaces, Part 2: Computer\nprocedures for the calculaƟon of light technical parameters for Category V and\nCategory P lighƟng,\n(v) AS/NZS 1158.3.1:2020, LighƟng for roads and public spaces, Part 3.1: Pedestrian\narea (Category P) lighƟng-Performance and design requirements,\n(vi) AS/NZS 1158.4:2015, LighƟng for roads and public spaces, Part 4: LighƟng of\npedestrian crossings,\n(vii) AS/NZS 1158.5:2014, LighƟng for roads and public spaces, Part 5: Tunnels and\nunderpasses.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":100},{"label":"24","code":null,"number":"24","title":"Use of driveways and parking areas","text":"(1) All driveways and parking areas must be unobstructed at all Ɵmes.\n(2) Except as otherwise permiƩed by an exisƟng condiƟon of the most recent development\nconsent (other than a complying development cerƟﬁcate) that applies to the premises,\ndriveways and car spaces-\n(a) must not be used for the manufacture, storage or display of goods, materials or\nany other equipment, and\n(b) must be used solely for vehicular access and for the parking of vehicles associated\nwith the use of the premises.\n46\n24A Loading and unloading of goods or materials\n(1) Loading and unloading goods or materials must not be carried out on a public road.\n(2) This condiƟon does not apply to a complying development cerƟﬁcate for development\ninvolving a building with a gross ﬂoor area less than 500m2 in Zone E1, E2, MU1, B1, B2, B3,\nB4, B8 or SP5 that existed before 1 February 2022.\n24B (Repealed)","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":100},{"label":"25","code":null,"number":"25","title":"Landscaped area (planƟng and maintenance)","text":"(1) Any tree or shrub that fails to establish within 2 years of the iniƟal planƟng date must be\nreplaced with the same species of tree or shrub.\n(2) All landscaped areas on the site must be maintained on an on-going basis.\nPART 5 CONDITIONS APPLYING TO ENTERTAINMENT VENUES","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":101},{"label":"26","code":null,"number":"26","title":"ApplicaƟon of prescribed condiƟons of development consent","text":"The condiƟons of a development consent for the use of a building as an entertainment venue\nprescribed for the purposes of the Act, s 4.17(11) are condiƟons of a complying development\ncerƟﬁcate for the use of a building as an entertainment venue.\nNote: See the Environmental Planning and Assessment RegulaƟon 2021, SecƟon 72.\nFire Safety Statement\nPart 12 of the Environmental Planning and Assessment (Development\nCertification and Fire Safety) Regulation 2021\n68 Willoughby Road, Crows Nest NSW 2065\nVersion 4 | Effective from 1 September 2022 | NSW Department of Planning and Environment | 1\nPlease note:\nInformation to assist building owners to complete each section of the statement is provided on pages 3, 4 and 5.\nSection 1: Type of statement\nThis is (mark applicable box): ☒ an annual fire safety statement (complete the declaration at Section 8 of this form)\n☐ a supplementary fire safety statement (complete the declaration at Section 9 of this form)\nSection 2: Description of the building or part of the building\nThis statement applies to: ☒ the whole building ☐ part of the building\nAddress (Street No., Street Name, Suburb and Postcode)\n68 Willoughby Road, Crows Nest NSW 2065\nLot No. (if known) DP/SP (if known) Building name (if applicable)\nFolio 20/617114\nProvide a brief description of the building or part (building use, number of storeys, construction type etc)\nRetail Shop with Basement\nSection 3: Name and address of the owner(s) of the building or part of the building\nFull Name (Given Name/s and Family Name) *\nJetrust Pty Ltd\n* Where the owner is not a person/s but an entity including a company or trust insert the full name of that entity.\nAddress (Street No., Street Name, Suburb and Postcode)\nC/- Henderson & Horning Pty Ltd, Level 5, 398 Sussex Street, Sydney NSW 2000\nSection 4: Fire safety measures\nFire safety measure Minimum standard of performance Date(s) assessed APFS *\nExit Lighting EA.5WSWE46 E48AS 2293.1 4-4-2025 F052489A\n* See notes on page 4 about how to correctly identify an accredited practitioner (fire safety) (APFS). Also, new rows can be added if required.\nSection 5: Inspection of fire exits and paths of travel to fire exits (Part 15)\nPart of the building inspected Date(s) inspected APFS *\nWhole of Premises Inspected and Comply 4-4-2025 F052489A\n* See notes on page 4 about how to correctly identify an accredited practitioner (fire safety) (APFS). Also, new rows can be added if required.\nSection 6: Name and contact details of each accredited practitioner (fire safety) (APFS)*\nFull name (Given Name/s and Family Name) Address Phone APFS* Signature\nJohn Moussa 36 Bristol Street Merrylands 2130\nJ2moussa@bigpond.com\n0466716058 F052489A\n* Where applicable - see notes on page 4 for further information.\nSection 7: Details of the person making the declaration in section 8 or 9 #\nFull name (Given Name/s and Family Name)\n68 Willoughby Road, Crows Nest NSW 2065\nVersion 4 | Effective from 1 September 2022 | NSW Department of Planning and Environment | 2\nFire Safety Statement\nPart 15 of the Environmental Planning and Assessment (Development\nCertification and Fire Safety) Regulation 2021\nOrganisation (if applicable) Title/Position (if applicable)\nAddress (Street No, Street Name, Suburb and Postcode)\nPhone Email\n# The person making the declaration in section 8 or 9 must not be an APFS listed in section 6 or their employer/employee or direct associate.\nSection 8: Annual fire safety statement declaration\nI, Click here (insert full name) being the: ☐ owner ☐ owner’s agent\ndeclare that:\na) each essential fire safety measure specified in this statement has been assessed by an accredited practitioner (fire\nsafety) as capable of performing:\ni. for an essential fire safety measure specified in the fire safety schedule, to a standard no less than that specified\nin the schedule, or\nii. for an essential fire safety measure applicable to the building but not specified in the fire safety schedule, to a\nstandard no less than that to which the measure was originally designed and implemented, and\nb) the building has been inspected by an accredited practitioner (fire safety) and was found, when it was inspected, to be\nin a condition that did not disclose grounds for a prosecution under Part 15 of the Regulation.\nOwner/Agent Signature Date issued\nSection 9: Supplementary fire safety statement declaration\nI, Click here (insert full name) being the: ☐ owner ☐ owner’s agent\ndeclare that each critical fire safety measure specified in this statement has been assessed by an accredited practitioner\n(fire safety) as capable of performing to at least the standard required by the current fire safety schedule for the building.\nOwner/Agent Signature Date issued\nNote:\n• A fire safety statement for a building must not be issued unless the statement is accompanied by a fire safety\nschedule for the building in accordance with the Regulation.\n• The building owner(s) are also responsible for ensuring that essential fire safety measures are maintained in\naccordance with section 81 of the Regulation. An agent cannot be made responsible for this requirement.\nWilliam j Rees\nx\n9/4/2025\nWilliam J Rees as Agent for Jetrust Pty Ltd\nHenderson & Horning Pty Ltd\nDirector\nLevel 5, 398 Sussex St, Sydney\n02 8217 8888\nwrees@hendersonhorning.com.au\n68 Willoughby Road, Crows Nest NSW 2065\nVersion 4 | Effective from 1 September 2022 | NSW Department of Planning and Environment | 3\nFire Safety Statement\nInformation to help building owners complete the Fire Safety Statement form\nPlease note:\nThe following information has been provided to help building owners complete the fire safety statement template\nand does not comprise part of the form. The following pages do not have to be displayed in the building and\nneed not be submitted to the local council and the Commissioner of Fire and Rescue NSW.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":101}]});
